I'm no authority on this, but I'm pretty sure you can't pull "ko'a .e ko'e"
out of the lu'i construct, as the job of "lu'i" is to convert it's
argument[s] into a new thing, which is the set containing the argument[s].

"lu'i ko'a" is a subset of "lu'i ko'a .e ko'e". It's not a two-way street,
in the same way that all circles are ellipses, but not all ellipses are
circles.

